+.. _cli_safe_import:
+
+Safe imports and table invariants
+================================
+
+Use ``--safe-mode`` with ``insert``, ``upsert`` or ``bulk`` to make the entire
+operation atomic, including all batches, table creation, added columns, indexes,
+triggers and CSV type detection. A write, parsing, conversion or validation error
+rolls back the operation and exits with status 1. Successful operations exit 0.
+Ordinary imports retain their existing batch commit behavior.
+
+Safe operations enable checkpoint support automatically. You can also enable or
+disable manual checkpoint support explicitly::
+
+    sqlite-utils enable-safe-import data.db
+    sqlite-utils disable-safe-import data.db
+
+Register persistent invariants before importing. Each command prints an opaque
+identity; pass that exact text to remove the invariant::
+
+    sqlite-utils add-import-invariant data.db products 'price >= 0'
+    sqlite-utils add-import-invariant data.db products 'COUNT(*) <= 10000'
+    sqlite-utils add-import-invariant data.db products 'SELECT SUM(price) >= 0 FROM products'
+    sqlite-utils list-import-invariants data.db products
+    sqlite-utils remove-import-invariant data.db products INVARIANT_ID
+
+``list-import-invariants`` prints JSON containing each ``id`` and original SQL
+``expression``. Invariants survive closing and reopening the database, and may be
+registered before their target table exists. SQL errors become identified
+validation failures. A scalar expression must be truthy for every row; an
+aggregate expression is evaluated once by SQLite. A ``SELECT`` query uses only
+the first column of its first row; an empty SELECT is false. Scalar expressions
+on empty tables pass, while aggregate expressions still evaluate their result.
+NULL is false. Use a full SELECT for joins, subqueries or grouped checks.
+
+Validate without changing any data::
+
+    sqlite-utils validate-import-invariants data.db products
+
+This command always exits 0 for a completed validation, including failed
+invariants or invalid invariant SQL. Its output says ``Validation passed`` or
+``Validation failed`` and includes the failing identities, expressions and error
+details. Invalid command arguments or an inaccessible database remain ordinary
+CLI errors. Use the JSON ``valid`` field to decide whether validation passed.
+
+Safe writes validate all registered invariants in the database before committing,
+so trigger writes to other tables are checked too. An already failing invariant
+on another table will also reject the operation::
+
+    sqlite-utils insert data.db products products.json --pk id --safe-mode
+    sqlite-utils insert data.db products products.csv --csv --alter --safe-mode
+    sqlite-utils upsert data.db products changes.json --pk id --safe-mode
+    sqlite-utils bulk data.db 'UPDATE products SET price = :price WHERE id = :id' changes.json --safe-mode
+
+JSON is the default format. The existing ``--csv``, ``--tsv`` and ``--nl`` flags
+select other formats. ``--batch-size`` still controls processing chunks in safe
+mode, but those chunks are committed together. ``--strict`` on CLI insert/upsert
+continues to mean SQLite STRICT table creation; failures in CLI safe mode always
+produce a nonzero exit status.
+
+Safe import support uses the reserved tables ``_sqlite_utils_import_settings``
+and ``_sqlite_utils_import_invariants``. Do not edit them directly. Checkpoints
+use SQLite transactions, not persistent recovery snapshots; process termination
+rolls back pending writes using SQLite's normal journal recovery. As with any
+SQLite transaction, external files and side effects of Python conversion code or
+SQL functions are outside the rollback boundary.
