+
+## Analysis before implementation
+
+Observable issue: normal imports commit batches independently; later insertion, conversion or invariant errors can leave earlier writes and schema changes committed. Direct trigger effects and type conversion must be atomic with the operation. Root cause: sqlite3 connection contexts commit on success and roll back on failure, regardless of an external savepoint. executescript also commits pending transactions. A wrapper savepoint without addressing these paths cannot protect an import.
+
+Impact: Database/Table transaction contexts, Database.executescript, persistent metadata, the common CLI importer and docs. Existing public ordinary write behavior remains the contract when no checkpoint is active. Checkpoints are connection-local savepoints, persistent configuration/invariants use two reserved SQLite tables. No existing data format is migrated; metadata is created lazily without rewriting user tables. Direct caller connection commit/rollback and transaction-control SQL remain caller-owned and cannot safely coexist with active checkpoints; this boundary will be documented.
+
+## Implementation plan
+
+1. Introduce named checkpoint and validation errors, persistent enable/invariant APIs and nested savepoint lifecycle. Finalizing an ancestor finalizes descendants; cleaning an active checkpoint rolls it back before removing its identity.
+2. Replace package-owned connection contexts with a shared checkpoint-aware transaction context. Preserve script statements without implicit commit while checkpoints are active. Do not introduce database snapshots or shadow user data.
+3. Execute SELECT invariants using the first value; execute expressions as SELECT(expression) FROM table and require every returned row to be truthy. SQLite naturally emits one row for aggregates. Preserve SQL errors as identified failures.
+4. Integrate one safe-operation runner into insert/upsert/CSV/JSON and CLI import/bulk. Validate all registered tables so triggers/cross-table changes are covered. Keep strict rollback-and-raise and non-strict structured failures. Explicit safe operations enable safe support when needed; manual checkpoint creation requires enablement.
